west africa's regional alliance ecuas  meeting in the ghanaian capital accra  the issue being discussed what if any  new sanctions should be imposed on mali  because of delays to its democratic  transition ecowas chairman and president  of ghana made his view clear in his  opening statement  it is my expectation  that we will take the appropriate  decisions that will advance the future  of mali  and our community  national regional and international  stakeholders  are all awaiting the result of our  meeting  the leaders announced a raft of new  sanctions on mali including border  closures suspending financial  transactions and withdrawing their  ambassadors the measures are in response  to mali's military leaders saying they  plan to stay in power for four more  years instead of holding elections next  month  as much as we are aware of the  complexity of the situation in this  country we are convinced that all  political economic and social reforms  aimed at rebuilding of mali could only  be carried out by democratically elected  authorities  when junta leader colonel assimi guerta  was sworn in last july following two  military coups in less than a year he  sought to unite malians and promised  presidential elections would be held  this year but then two weeks ago a  conference charged with recommending an  election timetable said the polls should  be delayed by between six months and  five years  ecowas has already imposed sanctions on  malian officials so will a new round  make any difference some analysts are  skeptical so i would have thought that  requires leaders would have used a much  more  nuanced engaged approach  with the malian leaders i i'm not too  sure  and particularly  conveys that the sanctions regime  is going to be very successful  mali's leaders say elections can't be  held next month because of security  concerns they also want time to draft a  new constitution and put it before  voters in a referendum ekwa says the  delays are unacceptable it's activated  its standby force and says it will be  ready for any eventuality  victoria gatenby al jazeera
 

